That's progress! A 500 means the route is now being hit. The error:

db is not defined

means exactly what it says: you don't have a variable named db.

From the code you shared earlier, you're using the MongoDB client directly, like this:

const collection = client.db("allAcounts").collection("acounts");

So your DELETE route should do the same instead of using db:

app.delete("/deleteAccount", async (req, res) => {
    try {
        const { _id } = req.query;

        const collection = client.db("allAcounts").collection("acounts");

        const result = await collection.deleteOne({
            _id: new ObjectId(_id),
        });

        if (result.deletedCount === 0) {
            return res.status(404).json({
                message: "Account not found",
            });
        }

        res.status(200).json({
            message: "Account deleted successfully",
        });
    } catch (error) {
        res.status(500).json({
            message: error.message,
        });
    }
});

One more thing I noticed: in your GET route you call:

await client.connect();

If your DELETE route doesn't call client.connect(), make sure you've already connected the client elsewhere when your app starts. If not, you'll need to connect before using the collection, just as you do in your GET route.

